# OpenRouter Mini — Architecture Analysis

## TL;DR — Single API, Not Multiple

**Your project creates a SINGLE unified API endpoint** (`POST /api/v1/chat/completions`) that routes to multiple LLM providers behind the scenes. This is exactly how the real OpenRouter works — one API, many models.

---

## Monorepo Structure

```
openrouter/                   ← Turborepo monorepo (Bun)
├── apps/
│   ├── api-backend/          ← LLM proxy (port 4000) — the core
│   ├── primary-backend/      ← Dashboard API (port 3000)
│   └── dashboard-frontend/   ← UI
├── packages/
│   ├── db/                   ← Prisma + PostgreSQL
│   ├── ui/                   ← Shared UI components
│   ├── eslint-config/
│   └── typescript-config/
```

---

## How It Works — Request Flow

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Client
    participant API as api-backend<br/>POST /api/v1/chat/completions
    participant DB as PostgreSQL
    participant LLM as LLM Provider

    Client->>API: { model: "google/gemini-2.0-flash", messages: [...] }
    API->>DB: Validate API key (Bearer token)
    API->>DB: Check user credits > 0
    API->>DB: Look up model by slug
    API->>DB: Get providers for this model (ModelProviderMapping)
    Note over API: Randomly pick a provider if multiple exist
    API->>LLM: Forward request to Gemini / OpenAI / Claude
    LLM-->>API: Response + token usage
    API->>DB: Deduct credits from user
    API->>DB: Track credits consumed on API key
    API-->>Client: Unified response format
```

### Key Steps in [index.ts](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/api-backend/src/index.ts):

1. **Parse model slug** — splits `"google/gemini-2.0-flash"` into company + model name
2. **Validate API key** — looks up [ApiKey](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/primary-backend/src/modules/apiKeys/service.ts#36-53) in DB, checks not disabled/deleted
3. **Check credits** — ensures user has credits remaining
4. **Resolve model** — finds the [Model](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/primary-backend/src/modules/models/service.ts#5-23) record by slug
5. **Pick a provider** — randomly selects from `ModelProviderMapping` entries (load balancing)
6. **Route to adapter** — dispatches to [Gemini](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/api-backend/src/llms/Gemini.ts#10-33), [OpenAi](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/api-backend/src/llms/OpenAi.ts#8-31), or [Claude](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/api-backend/src/llms/Claude.ts#11-37) class based on provider name
7. **Calculate cost** — [(inputTokens × inputCost + outputTokens × outputCost) / 10](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/primary-backend/src/app.ts#13-14)
8. **Deduct credits** — decrements user credits and increments API key usage tracking

---

## LLM Adapter Pattern

All adapters extend [BaseLlm](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/api-backend/src/llms/Base.ts) and return a unified [LlmResponse](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/api-backend/src/llms/Base.ts#3-14):

| Adapter | SDK | Provider Names |
|---|---|---|
| [Gemini.ts](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/api-backend/src/llms/Gemini.ts) | `@google/genai` | "Google API", "Google Vertex" |
| [Claude.ts](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/api-backend/src/llms/Claude.ts) | `@anthropic-ai/sdk` | "Claude API" |
| [OpenAi.ts](file:///c:/Users/siddh/Brain%20Hop%20complete/openrouter/apps/api-backend/src/llms/OpenAi.ts) | `openai` | "OpenAI" |

Each adapter normalizes the provider-specific response into:
```ts
{
  completions: { choices: [{ message: { content: string } }] },
  inputTokensConsumed: number,
  outputTokensConsumed: number
}
```

---

## Database Schema

```mermaid
erDiagram
    User ||--o{ ApiKey : "has many"
    User ||--o{ OnrampTransaction : "has many"
    User ||--o{ Conversation : "has many"
    Company ||--o{ Model : "has many"
    Model ||--o{ ModelProviderMapping : "available via"
    Provider ||--o{ ModelProviderMapping : "serves"
    ApiKey ||--o{ Conversation : "used in"
    ModelProviderMapping ||--o{ Conversation : "routed through"
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The **`ModelProviderMapping`** table is the key to the routing. A single model (e.g. GPT-4) can map to multiple providers, each with different token costs. The API randomly picks one at request time.

---

## Dashboard API (`primary-backend` on port 3000)

Separate Elysia app with 4 modules:

| Module | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **auth** | Signup/signin with password hashing (`Bun.password.hash`), JWT |
| **apiKeys** | Create (`sk-or-v1-...`), list, enable/disable, soft-delete API keys |
| **models** | List available models, providers, and their mappings |
| **payments** | Credit onramp (+1000 credits per transaction, uses DB transactions) |

---

## Summary

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| **Single or multiple APIs?** | **Single endpoint** — `POST /api/v1/chat/completions` |
| **How does routing work?** | Model slug → DB lookup → random provider selection → adapter dispatch |
| **What providers?** | Google (Gemini), Anthropic (Claude), OpenAI |
| **How are costs tracked?** | Per-token costs stored in `ModelProviderMapping`, deducted from user credits |
| **How are keys formatted?** | `sk-or-v1-{20 random chars}` |
| **Tech stack** | Bun + Elysia + Prisma + PostgreSQL + Turborepo |
